  "response": "## What the scan actually shows for these three files\n\nFiltering the 92 findings to the named trio, the \"petty agent-risk\" cluster is 8 findings — but they don't distribute the way the brief implies:\n\n| Type | src/index.ts | src/rollup.ts | src/esbuild/svelte.ts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `name_behavior_mismatch` | `crime_00016` (build), `crime_00020` (buildAll) | `crime_00004` (getRollupConfig), `crime_00011` (buildStart) | — |\n| `option_bag_junk_drawer` | — | `crime_00008` (getRollupConfig) | — |\n| `return_shape_roulette` | — | — | `crime_00017/18/19` |\n\nTwo corrections before the plan:\n\n- **The three svelte.ts findings are one piece of code.** `sveltePlugin` (9–124) contains `setup` (17–122) contains the `onLoad` callback (31–121). The detector counts the same return statements at all three nesting levels. Three IDs, one fix.\n- **The repo's worst option bags aren't in these files.** `runEsbuild` reads 38 distinct properties (`crime_00023`); `main` in `cli-main.ts` reads 8 (`crime_00005`). If option-bag tightening is the goal, `src/esbuild/index.ts` is the bigger prize.\n\n---\n\n## Group A — False Identity (4 findings, 3 different correct answers)\n\nThe detector fires on the same pattern four times; the right response differs each time.\n\n**`crime_00004` — `getRollupConfig` (`src/rollup.ts:38`). Real, and worse than reported.** The scan cites `setSilent, removeFiles`. `setSilent` is accurate — `src/rollup.ts:41` mutates module-level state in `log.ts` before any config is built. `removeFiles` is a lexical over-attribution: it lives in `tsupCleanPlugin.buildStart` (77–81), which `getRollupConfig` only *constructs*. But the underlying risk is understated, not overstated — a function named `get*` returns an object that deletes `**/*.d.{ts,mts,cts}` when rollup later runs it.\n\nWhat the detector misses entirely: **`getRollupConfig` mutates its argument.** `src/rollup.ts:45` aliases rather than copies (`const dtsOptions = options.dts || {}`), then lines 46 and 49 write through that alias into the caller's `options.dts`.\n\nProposal — make the name true rather than renaming:\n1. Hoist `setSilent(options.silent)` up to `startRollup` (216).\n2. `const dtsOptions = { ...(options.dts ?? {}) }` — stop writing through the caller's object.\n3. Extract the clean plugin's body to a named `cleanDtsOutput(outDir)` so the destructive call is greppable.\n\n**`crime_00011` — `buildStart` (`src/rollup.ts:77`). Real, but cannot be renamed.** `buildStart` is a rollup `Plugin` lifecycle hook; the name is dictated by the interface. The suggested action (\"rename to match behaviour\") is unavailable — extraction is the only move, which is item 3 above.\n\n**`crime_00016` / `crime_00020` — `build`, `buildAll` (`src/index.ts:167`, `291`). Suppress, don't fix.** `build` is the package's primary export (`export async function build`). In a bundler, \"build\" *means* \"perform side effects\"; the heuristic (\"name prefix suggests a pure transformation\") is misfiring on domain vocabulary, and renaming a public export to satisfy it is a breaking change for negative value. Allowlist both.\n\n---\n\n## Group B — Option Bag Junk Drawer (`crime_00008`)\n\nThe summary says \"implicit shape,\" which isn't quite right — the parameter *is* typed (`NormalizedOptions`, `src/rollup.ts:39`). The real cost is that `NormalizedOptions` is `Omit<MarkRequired<Options, …>>` over a ~165-line `Options` type, so the signature tells a reader nothing about which of ~50 fields this function depends on. It reads 10.\n\nThe concrete hazard is at **`src/index.ts:222–238`**. Because `options` is one god-object, crossing the worker boundary requires a hand-maintained deny-list nulling out `injectStyle`, `banner`, `footer`, `esbuildPlugins`, `esbuildOptions`, `plugins`, `treeshake`, `onSuccess`, `outExtension` — everything function-valued, because functions can't be structured-cloned.\n\n> **This is the thing an agent gets wrong today.** Add one function-valued option to `Options`, wire it through `normalizeOptions`, ship it — and the dts worker throws `DataCloneError` at runtime whenever `dts` is enabled. Nothing in any type says the list at 225–238 exists, and `rollup.ts`'s tests are weak (`test_gap 0.75`), so nothing catches it.\n\nProposal: define the projection the worker boundary already implies.\n\n```ts\n// src/options.ts\n/** The subset of NormalizedOptions that survives structuredClone\n    and is actually read by the dts worker. */\nexport type DtsBuildOptions = Pick<NormalizedOptions,\n  | 'cjsInterop' | 'clean' | 'dts' | 'entry' | 'external'\n  | 'format' | 'outDir' | 'outExtension' | 'silent' | 'tsconfig'>\n```\n\nThen `getRollupConfig(options: DtsBuildOptions)` and build the `postMessage` payload with an explicit `toDtsBuildOptions(options)` projection. The deny-list becomes compiler-enforced instead of a comment, and the signature documents the contract.\n\n---\n\n## Group C — Return Shape Roulette (`crime_00017/18/19`)\n\nThe `fix_shape` here — \"pick one return shape\" — is wrong for this code, and following it would break the plugin. The \"6 shapes\" span three *different* esbuild callbacks with three legitimately different contracts:\n\n- `src/esbuild/svelte.ts:22` → `OnResolveResult` (`{ path, namespace }`)\n- `:92` → svelte-preprocess `Processed` (`{ code, map }`)\n- `:37`, `:117`, `:119` → `OnLoadResult`, which is genuinely a union: `{ errors }` and `{ contents, warnings }` are both valid\n\nSo this is largely a **measurement artifact of missing annotations**, not a design defect. The fix is to annotate, letting esbuild's own types carry the union:\n\n```ts\nbuild.onResolve({ filter: /\\.svelte\\.css$/ }, (args): OnResolveResult => …)\nbuild.onLoad({ filter: /\\.svelte$/ }, async (args): Promise<OnLoadResult> => …)\n```\n\nThat should clear all three findings at once.\n\nTwo real traps the detector didn't catch, worth fixing in the same pass:\n\n1. **`convertMessage` takes `any` (`:43`) and is fed a caught `unknown` (`:119`).** Anything thrown by `readFile` or `esbuild.transform` — not just Svelte compiler errors — lands there. Non-Svelte errors have no `start`/`end`, so `location` silently goes `undefined` and the error loses its source position. Type it `(e: unknown): PartialMessage` and force the narrowing.\n2. **`convertMessage` closes over `source`, which is declared 18 lines *later* (`:61`).** It works only because both call sites (`:117`, `:119`) run after initialization. An agent moving the `readFile` inside the `try` — an obvious-looking improvement, since that call can fail — gets a TDZ `ReferenceError: Cannot access 'source' before initialization`. Pass `source` as a parameter instead of capturing it.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested order\n\n1. **`DtsBuildOptions` + explicit worker projection** (Group B). Highest real risk, and it makes the rest mechanical.\n2. **`getRollupConfig` effects** — hoist `setSilent`, clone `options.dts`, extract `cleanDtsOutput` (Group A, `crime_00004`/`00011`).\n3. **Annotate the svelte callbacks + fix `convertMessage`** (Group C). Independent of 1–2; can run in parallel.\n4. **Allowlist `build`/`buildAll`** via `detectors.options` rather than renaming public API.\n\nOne caveat on sequencing: `src/rollup.ts` has `test_gap 0.75` and the suite carries 12 zero-assertion tests (`crime_00057`–`00068`), so there is no safety net under steps 1–2. I'd add an assertion that a dts build with a function-valued option doesn't throw `DataCloneError` *before* touching the worker boundary.\n\nSteps 1–3 leave the six `large_function` findings across these files untouched — `getRollupConfig` is 122 lines, `build` is 293. Those are a separate, larger piece of work; I'd not bundle them into a typing-and-naming pass.",
